Duranium USB debug layer (duranium-usb-debug)
=============================================

What it is
----------
A development-only debug layer for postmarketOS (Duranium) images, from
https://github.com/exynos-linux/usb-debug. It brings up a USB NCM network
interface and exposes, over that interface only:

  * register console on TCP 23   -> usb-debug-console: peek/poke/dump, cat,
                                    put, run, insmod, i2c, reboot ... (the
                                    same console as the exynos-linux
                                    bring-up init; docs/PROTOCOL.md)
  * root shell on TCP 2323       -> busybox "telnetd -l /bin/sh" (no auth)
  * ssh on TCP 22                -> a dedicated sshd (its own host key)
  * kernel log on TCP 24         -> "dmesg -w", or /dev/kmsg without util-linux
  * adb (duranium-usb-debug-adb) -> adbd on FunctionFS: adb shell/push/pull

Fixed addressing, the same on every device and in the bring-up init, so
the host tool works unchanged:

  * gadget VID:PID 0x1d6b:0x0104
  * device MAC 02:57:0a:00:00:01  -> device link-local  fe80::57:aff:fe00:1
  * host   MAC 02:57:0a:00:00:02  -> host link-local     fe80::57:aff:fe00:2

Host usage: the "usb-debug" command (same repository), or by hand
(replace usb0 with the host's cdc_ncm interface):

  usb-debug cmd dmesg                    # register console command
  usb-debug shell                        # root shell
  nc  fe80::57:aff:fe00:1%usb0 2323      # the same, raw
  nc  fe80::57:aff:fe00:1%usb0 24 | less # kernel log
  ssh root@fe80::57:aff:fe00:1%usb0      # needs a root key or password first

All of them are socket-activated (systemd .socket units with Accept=yes),
listening on the wildcard address but bound to the gadget interface with
SO_BINDTODEVICE (BindToDevice=usb0). That works before the link has carrier
and before IPv6 duplicate address detection finished, which binding the
link-local address directly does not.

It is present both in the initrd (interface, console, shell and kernel log;
-initramfs subpackage) and in the booted system (adds sshd, which needs the
real root). The gadget stays bound across switch-root.

Settings
--------
KEY=value files in /usr/lib/duranium-usb-debug/conf.d/ (device packages)
and /etc/duranium-usb-debug/conf.d/ (local admin): the USB device controller
(UDC=), the ports (CONSOLE_PORT=, SHELL_PORT=, SSH_PORT=, KLOG_PORT=; 0 turns
a service off) and the gadget's USB strings. See usb-debug.conf.example in
this directory. duranium-usb-debug-generator applies the ports at boot.

SECURITY
--------
This is an UNAUTHENTICATED root shell and register console, and an SSH
daemon that permits root login, reachable by anyone who can attach to the
USB port. It is for development images only. It listens on the USB gadget
interface only and the firewall (60_duranium_usb_debug.nft) only opens its
ports on usb*, but USB access is physical root access regardless. Do not
ship it in a release image.

How to disable it
-----------------
Booted system, whole layer off (the target's Also= list covers every unit):
    systemctl disable --now duranium-usb-debug.target
Permanently, so a preset cannot re-enable it:
    systemctl mask duranium-usb-debug.target duranium-usb-debug.service \
        duranium-debug-console.socket duranium-debug-shell.socket \
        duranium-debug-sshd.socket duranium-debug-klog.socket
Just one piece, e.g. keep the interface but drop the shell:
    systemctl disable --now duranium-debug-shell.socket
The initrd's units come from presets baked into the UKI and cannot be
disabled at runtime; the kernel command line switch covers the initrd and the
booted system alike:
    duranium.usb_debug=0

Release images
--------------
This layer is a SEPARATE package. A release image is built without it (no
duranium-usb-debug in the image's package list), so no masking is needed.
